 \par
\par
This island's mine, by Sycorax my mother, \par
Which thou takest from me\'85 \par
\par
 \par
\par
For the modern audience, these are surely the central lines in this passage. Modern directors tend to find a means of highlighting this centrality \f1\endash  in the 2013 production at the Globe theatre, for example, James Garnon \endash  who played Caliban \endash  yelled the lines, placing a huge emphasis on the second stressed syllable of the first line so that the word \ldblquote mine\rdblquote  was a gigantic screech that could be heard beyond the confines of the theatre. Such an emphasis is hardly surprising given the fact that for the last 70 years or so, our view of The Tempest has been influenced hugely by post-colonial views of the text (Octave Mennoni\rquote s hugely influential text Prospero and Caliban: The Psychology of Colonization was first published in 1950).  \par
\par
 \par
\par
It is undeniable that a modern audience is attuned not only to the righteous outrage of Caliban, but also to the fact that Prospero\rquote s language in this passage is shot through with ghastly threats that he will torture his servant and that he seems unable to refer to Caliban with anything other than pejorative language: Caliban is his \ldblquote slave\rdblquote ; he is also a \ldblquote tortoise\rdblquote  (presumably on the basis of being slow to meet his master\rquote s demands); he is a \ldblquote\'85poisonous slave, got by the devil himself/Upon thy wicked dam\'85\rdblquote  (Prospero\rquote s verbal assault on Caliban\rquote s mother is bound to be particularly upsetting for the islander); this embodiment of \ldblquote malice\rdblquote  is, it seems, finally, \ldblquote filth\rdblquote . What strikes the ear of the modern viewer of the play is not the incorrigible wickedness of Caliban (elsewhere in the play he is described as by Prospero as \ldblquote\'85A devil, a born devil, on whose nature/Nurture can never stick\'85\rdblquote  IV.i.188-189), but the consistent \ldblquote filthiness\rdblquote  of Prospero\rquote s own language. This is the language, of course, that is necessary if one is to torture a fellow human being: we cannot help but notice that those who are tortured tend to be dehumanized first. Shakespeare in this passage seems, remarkably, to have understood this lesson from History.   \par
\par
 \par
\par
Yet as is acknowledged in this passage, the relationship between Prospero and Caliban was not always dysfunctional: Caliban notes that \ldblquote\'85When thou camest first,/Thou strokedst me and madest much of me\'85\rdblquote  While Prospero and his daughter obviously saw it as their responsibility to educate the indigenous islander (\ldblquote\'85I pitied thee,/Took pains to make thee speak, taught thee each hour/One thing or other\'85\rdblquote  says Miranda), Caliban too sought to teach the Milanese aristocrats how to cope on the island by showing them \ldblquote the qualities o' the isle\rdblquote . It is obvious what brought this prelapsarian idyll to an end: both Prospero and Miranda claim that Caliban tried to sexually assault the latter. A relationship characterised by mutual tolerance seems to have been destroyed when Caliban sought \ldblquote to violate/The honour of my child.\rdblquote  Alarmingly, some critics seem to regard the attempted rape as \ldblquote inevitable\rdblquote  - Katherine Duncan-Jones makes this bizarre claim in the In Our Time episode on the play. More ethically acceptable is the suggestion that this attempted \ldblquote violation\rdblquote  might be a product of a catastrophic cultural misunderstanding: aristocratic 17th century courtship rituals were incredibly elaborate and both parties involved are sexually inexperienced \endash  given this, one can easily understand why there might have been a disastrous miscommunication between Miranda and Caliban (such a reading would require us to regard Caliban\rquote s response to the charge as wholly rhetorical, constituting a forceful \ldblquote doubling down\rdblquote  on the lie that he is a frustrated rapist). It is difficult to see, though, how such a reading of the play could be represented in a production \endash  it might also be an unwise interpretation of this scene given the way in which this reading ignores the plaintive voice of a vulnerable young woman (a process that we see replicated regularly in the nation\rquote s law courts).     \par
\par
 \par
\par
It is certainly the case that this scene sees the introduction of a character who has gone from being viewed as \ldblquote a savage and deformed slave\rdblquote  (this description, it must be remembered, is that of Ralph Crane, the play\rquote s compositor, not that of Shakespeare) to being one of the most complex and interesting characters in the whole play. The Tempest has, latterly, become something of a political battleground and Caliban is at the centre of this conflict. Time and again in the play his utterances open up huge areas for interpretation: his claim to Prospero that \ldblquote You taught me language; and my profit on't/Is, I know how to curse\rdblquote  reveals the truth of the Wittgenstein insight that \ldblquote The limits of my language are the limits of my world\rdblquote  \endash  if Caliban\rquote s world is one full of pain and anguish then this is not, surely, a consequence of his own actions: in his claims in this passage that a terrible injustice has been done, the modern audience hears the voice of so many of History\rquote s victims.       \par
